Subject: Health Care Again

The 'isolated community' is and always has been a straw-man.  Almost
as far back as we have archeological evidence from civilizations,
man has somehow transported stuff across great distances.  There
are iron-age artifacts that bear evidence of a single source (
impurities in a metallic artifact) being spread a thousand miles
or more.  In the middle ages, silks and spices from China and
Indonesia mad it as far as Britain.  Yesterday, on my FAX machine,
was an offer to our food services (cafteria) of fish caught in
Alaskan waters, processed in China (cut up into pieces, deboned,
etc.), and stored in a warehouse in Atlanta, for sale to Huntsville,
AL at a competitive price.

In the case of Mr. Shapiro's previous message, for much less than
the cost of a brain operation, you can be carried by ambulance and
airplane from just about anywhere in the world to a major medical
center.  


For example, round trip from Taipei, Taiwan to Huntsville, AL
coach class, summer, is $1530 on Delta Airlines.  This is about 1/2
a day's stay in a hospital with surgery/intensive care thrown in.
The cost of transport from home to airport and airport to hospital
would be minor additions.  Now, I grant you, access to regular
checkups that will FIND a brain tumor in a timely fashion is a
different matter, since it is a repetitive operation.  There are
counties in rural Alabama that appaently have NO (none,zip) primary
care physicians.  But even in this extreme case, people get
medical care.  They just have to drive an hour or two to get to
a city with doctors.  This is a burden, and an incentive to move
closer to a city, but it is in no way even remotely close to
a case of 'no medical care available'.

In a free-er market, I would expect a class of intermediate
skill providers, between Registered Nurse and M.D., who would
be in greater supply than the highly overspecialized doctors we
have today.  What has happened is that with a restricted supply,
though medical school admission restrictions and licensing boards,
there are too few doctors.  Most nowadays go to the more lucrative
specialties, which have higher training requirements, and command
higher incomes.  The family practice, GP type jobs get the
remaining few doctors (less than 1/3 today).  So there is an
increasing shortage of regular GPs, and thus more of the standard
office tasks (taking blood pressure, etc.) are done by nurses.

This reduces the contact time of the physician with the patient,
and makes it harder for him to detect the signs of a problem,
which depends to some extent on knowing the quirks of each 
patient, and oberving subtle clues.

Dani Eder

