Date: Wed, 3 Feb 93 08:34:34 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Zero-sum games

I think there may be some problems with Robin's definition of zero-sum.
I worked out some examples of how a sample futures contract and stock
sale might work, which do seem to confirm his suggestions.  But as I will
discuss below, I wonder whether this definition is appropriate for the
present discussion.

Here is my model of how a futures contract works.  Let's suppose Robin
and I disagree about the future price of gold.  I say that one year
from now it will be less than $450/oz, while Robin believes that it
will be more.  (This doesn't necessarily represent my or Robin's true
views, other than by accident!)  Putting our money where our mouths
are, we agree on the following contract:

One year from now, I will deliver one ounce of gold to Robin in
exchange for $450.

I will execute this contract by waiting one year and then
buying one ounce of gold at the current market price, and delivering
it to Robin, who will pay me $450.  He will then turn around and sell
the gold for the current market price.  In practice, since I am buying
the gold and he is just going to turn around and sell it for the same
price (neglecting transaction costs), we may skip that step and just
pay each other the amount which would leave us in the same situation
as if we had actually done it.  For example, if gold turns out to be
selling for $500/oz in one year, I'd end up losing $50 on the deal,
and Robin would make $50.  So we'd just settle by having me pay Robin
$50.

I think this is essentially how actual futures contracts work, except
that a broker acts as a go-between, and he will require some money
up front to make sure you will honor your commitments.

Now, looking at the game theory matrix, with the two possibilities
being A=trade and B=don't trade, and supposing that the price of gold
is x dollars per ounce, the outcomes (me, Robin) will be:

		A		B
	A	(450-x,x-450)	(0,0)
	B	(0,0)		(0,0)

(Sorry, I don't feel up to Ascii graphics tonight!)  The only interesting
case is where we both want to make the transaction, A-A, and in that
case whatever one of us wins the other loses.

This is clearly a zero-sum game, if utilities are measured in dollars.

Now, for stocks, I'm not really sure how to model the corresponding
situation.  Here is what I've tried.  Suppose I own 100 shares of stock
currently worth $4.50 a share.  I am considering selling them to Robin
for that price.  In one year, the stock will be worth x dollars.  Again,
A=trade and B=don't trade:

		A		B
	A	(450,x-450)	(x,0)
	B	(x,0)		(x,0)

The justification is that for all but A-A, we don't trade, and I just
hold the stock, which is worth x.  If we trade Robin is out $450 but has
stock worth x, while I have $450.

Now, it does appear that by Robin's definition this is a zero-sum game.
If we subtract x from all of my payoffs we get:

		A		B
	A	(450-x,x-450)	(0,0)
	B	(0,0)		(0,0)

Does this look familiar?  It is the same payoff matrix as in the futures
contract example.  This is clearly zero-sum.  And the stock trade is
the equivalent of playing this game while I am given a side payment of
$x.

This similarity can be extended.  Go back to the futures contract and
suppose that instead of my buying the gold a year from now, I'll spend
the next year panning for gold to collect my ounce.  (Suppose I enjoy
panning for gold and that is how I like to spend my weekends.  I have
a friend, by the way, who often goes up to the mountains with his father
and they'll sometimes collect $50 worth of gold in a weekend.)

Now, the payoff matrix looks like:

		A		B
	A	(450,x-450)	(0,0)
	B	(0,0)		(0,0)

The idea is that if we agree on the contract, I'm going to get $450
at the end of the year.  Robin will still pay $450 and turn around and
sell the gold, so his payoff is unchanged.

Now, we have an identical payoff matrix to the stock trading example.
And, as in that case, it can still be made zero-sum in the game theory
sense.

So, this does seem to support Robin's claim that both the futures
market and the stock market are zero-sum, as far as the basic transactions
are concerned, using the game theory sense of the term.

Having gone through this analysis, though, I am convinced that the
game theory definition of zero-sum does not capture the differences
people have in mind when they compare the futures and stock markets,
saying "The futures market is zero-sum, but the stock market is better
because it's positive-sum."  (I think it was some claim like this which
started the whole debate.)

Game theory analyses strategies.  It looks at what considerations a
rational player would use in choosing between his various possible
strategies.  And from this perspective, a constant side payment really
is irrelevant.  From the game theory point of view, the following
three games are equivalent:

		A		B
	A	(110,90)	(96,104)
	B	(100,100)	(99,101)

		A		B
	A	(10,-10)	(-4,4)
	B	(0,0)		(-1,1)

		A		B
	A	(-90,-110)	(-104,-96)
	B	(-100,-100)	(-101,-99)

In each case, the optimal strategy is B-B.  Answering this question of
optimal strategies is exactly the purpose of game theory.

But in real life these games aren't equivalent.  The first one is
preferable.  There is more to consider than just the choice-of-strategy
which is analyzed by game theory.

This is what motivates us to describe games as positive-sum and
negative-sum, even though as Robin explained these terms have no
meaning in game theory.  But that doesn't mean that they have no
meaning in other contexts.  Looking at these three games, there is a real
sense in which the first is positive-sum, the second zero-sum, and
the third negative-sum.

Looking back at the futures market versus stock market, then, the first
two game matrices above described typical transactions in such markets.
In game theory terms, both are zero-sum because constant payments
are abstracted out.  But if we take those side payments into consideration,
the futures contract remains zero-sum, while the stock market becomes
(at least potentially) positive-sum.  (It could be negative-sum too,
sometimes, but historically stocks plus dividends have appreciated in
value.)

Now, as my last example showed, futures can also be postive-sum, if
you are one of the people actually making or taking delivery.  A farmer
who sells a futures contract for later delivery of spring wheat at a
fixed price will make a profit off that contract.  But such contracts
are a tiny fraction of the total number of futures transactions.  The
vast majority of futures contracts are settled the way Robin and I
settled our contract in the first example: by a simple cash payment
taken from the "loser" and paid to the "winner".

With stocks, on the other hand, the (potentially) positive-sum case
is very typical.  Considering both capital gains and dividends, stock
transactions are generally positive-sum.

This, I think, is what is meant when people say that the stock market
is positive-sum while futures trading is zero-sum.  Although there
are exceptions to every rule, most transactions seem to be in accordance
with this principle.

Hal

