Subject: I&A: Help Bust Entropic Patent

Do you sometimes wish that you could type at 120 w.p.m.? How 'bout 200 
w.p.m.? I posit that extropians will be concerned about increasing their 
typing speeds, especially as e-mail continues its radical increase in use and 
importance. Many more people would be typing at these speeds today, if not 
for an entropic software patent which I request extropian assistance in 
defeating (but don't worry, there won't be a pledge drive).

The company that holds this patent is Productivity Software International. 
They market a product called PRD+ which allows fast typing by automatically 
expanding abbreviations which are followed by a space, comma, exclamation 
point, question mark, etc. For example, typing a space after typing the 
letter "t" signals the program to create "the". Typing a comma after typing 
"ifdo" produces, "If there is anything I can do, ". The advantage over 
traditional macros that require the use of meta keys such as the CTRL ALT and 
function keys, is that your hands stay on the home row, your flow is not 
interrupted, and a mnemonic relationship makes it easy to memorize 
significant numbers of short forms. It is not uncommon for people to double 
their typing speeds using this technique, especially if they memorize a large 
number of short forms, and/or use a limited vocabulary with many stock 
sentences and phrases.

P.S.I.'s patent specifically covers using SPACE, COMMA, etc. to expand 
abbreviations - what I think of as the natural delimiters. If they 
aggresively improved and marketed their product, it wouldn't be so bad. 
However, the reality is that they don't advertise, have promised but not 
delivered upon Windows and Mac versions for over 2 years, and have proven 
difficult to enter into license arrangements with.

My interest, aside from my desire to be able to record my stream of thought 
in real time, is that abbreviation expansion is an important technique for 
speeding up input with chord keyboards. One of the companies that I have been 
working with on the Hip PC, Infogrip, Inc., is about to introduce a desktop 
chord keyboard to the market which will be called the BAT. They would like to 
include PRD-like functionality in their product, and have asked me to 
investigate the strength of P.S.I.'s patent. If they decide to fight it, the 
fact that their owner has incredibly deep pockets should make it all the 
easier.

Here's why I think it can be won, and how maybe some of you can help. I have 
heard and read that different unix word processors have supported this 
feature for years - notably EMACS. Is this true? Do any of you have examples 
of prior art?

Secondly, I believe this issue was discussed in a past issue off EFFector 
online. I do not have access to ftp, so searching for and getting a copy of 
this issue is problematic for me. If anyone can find this article, and 
forward it to me it would be greatly appreciated.

